Concurrent Fibonacci regression checks
======================================

  ./maxima-local --no-init --lisp=sbcl \
    --batch-string='run_testsuite(tests=[rtest_parallel_fibonacci]);'

Run every configured Lisp. Require all 11 examples and "No unexpected
errors"; Maxima can catch Lisp errors while returning process status zero.

The original FFIB keeps PREVFIB in a closure shared by every root call.
Overlapping computations can overwrite it and return incorrect integers.
FFIB now allocates that state per root and uses a local recursive function.
The doubling formulas, negative-index behavior, number of returned values
and public handling of non-fixnum arguments are unchanged.

The reference uses the defining addition/subtraction recurrence, independently
of the production doubling algorithm. It checks every index from -256 to 256,
128 generated indices from -2000 to 2000 and Cassini's identity, exact large
values, negative indices, symbolic arguments and the serial fallback.

Six batches of 48 adjacent indices starting at 20000 exercise ordinary
parallel scheduling. A second check starts two native workers with a bounded
rendezvous so each worker must claim one job item. Each computes six batches
of 32 exact values, compared with the independent reference. Cleanup attempts
every recorded join, including after partial startup failure. Without native
threads, that check returns true and the fallback tests still run.

Overlap inside the short recursive calculation depends on scheduling. Run
these checks repeatedly in fresh processes when investigating concurrency;
the rendezvous ensures two active workers, not a particular instruction
interleaving. No timing or throughput target is asserted.
